diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s routing table (FRR/zebra RIB/FIB) is missing a default route. Mechanism: Without a 0.0.0.0/0 route, the kernel only forwards to prefixes that have an explicit match (connected or RIP-learned). Any “external/unknown” destination that is not present in the RIP table lacks a next hop and is dropped, which matches the symptom that RIP-learned subnets work while everything else fails from router6.

Localization: Router6, uplink interface eth2 (10.50.1.2/24) toward router3 (expected next hop 10.50.1.1). Evidence: FRR shows no default route on router6 (show ip route lists only C and R routes; no 0.0.0.0/0). The Linux kernel routing table on router6 likewise lacks a default route (ip route output contains no “default via …”), while showing many RIP-learned specifics via 10.50.1.1. This confirms the control-plane programming of a default route is absent on router6.

Root cause: The static default route (0.0.0.0/0 via 10.50.1.1 on eth2) was removed from router6 during the config change.

Corrective fix: Reinstall the default route and persist it in FRR. For example: in FRR, configure “ip route 0.0.0.0/0 10.50.1.1” and write memory. If downstream LANs relied on learning a default from router6, also restore “router rip” → “redistribute static” or “default-information originate” as previously used. Verify by checking “show ip route” for S*> 0.0.0.0/0 via 10.50.1.1 and confirming router6 can ping external addresses. === END ===